Scene.description:
A view's content and behavior: a space, marks drawn in order, a layout, a theme, controls, an
ease duration, and an optional title.

A scene is plain data. In JSON, fields are camelCase and each mark is
`{ vertices | edges | faces | lines | areas: options }`.

```
# use latkit::{Colormap, Edges, Earth, Projection, Scene, Vertices, field};
let voltage = field("vm").colormap(Colormap::BALANCE).domain(0.95..=1.05);
let scene = Scene::new(Earth::default().projection(Projection::Globe))
    .draw(Edges::of("grid", "Branch").ends("from", "to"))
    .draw(Vertices::of("grid", "Bus").position("lon", "lat").color(voltage).legend("Bus voltage · pu"))
    .title("Voltage");
```

Shot.description:
A picture of views, taken once they are complete as its [`Capture`] says. Each view draws into
its panel of a `size`-pixel picture at `scale` pixels per CSS pixel. A shot is plain data, like
a scene.

```no_run
# async fn example(view: &mut latkit::View) -> latkit::Result<()> {
use latkit::Shot;
let image = Shot::new([3840, 2160]).scale(2.0).image(&mut [view]).await?;
std::fs::write("view.png", image.encode(latkit::ImageFormat::Png)?).expect("written");
# Ok(()) }
```

Panels.description:
The region of a shared target each view draws in, one panel per view, in order. A panel is
`[x, y, width, height]` as fractions of the target, from its top left. Later panels draw over
earlier ones. With no panels, one view fills the target.

```
use latkit::Panels;
let map_over_plot = Panels::from([[0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.8], [0.0, 0.8, 1.0, 0.2]]);
assert_eq!(map_over_plot.len(), 2);
assert_eq!(Panels::default().len(), 1);
```
